Onyx is licensed in two parts. The full texts are in LICENSES/.


CODE — crates/, packages/, scripts/, examples/

    MIT OR Apache-2.0, at your option.

    LICENSES/MIT.txt
    LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt

    "OR" means you choose one; you do not have to satisfy both. Apache-2.0 carries an
    express patent grant, which MIT does not. MIT is offered alongside it because
    Apache-2.0 is incompatible with GPLv2, and a GPLv2 project should still be able to
    use this code.


THE FORMAT — spec/, corpus/, migrations/, docs/

    CC0-1.0, a public domain dedication.

    LICENSES/CC0-1.0.txt

    The specification, the conformance corpus and the migration manifests carry no
    conditions at all. Implement them freely, fork them, rename them. An implementer has
    to copy the test fixtures to prove conformance, and any attribution requirement would
    be friction on exactly the thing this project wants people to do.

    CC0 waives copyright but does not grant patent rights, which is why it covers the
    data and never the code.


Published crates carry their own copies of the MIT and Apache-2.0 texts, because cargo
packages only what lives inside a crate directory. `node scripts/vendor.mjs --check`
verifies those copies are byte-identical to the originals here, and CI runs it.

